confirmed.description:
The count branch is tested before the explicit-id branch and passes nothing but the limit:

```python
count = params.get("count")
if count is not None:
    try:
        n = max(1, min(int(count), 20))
    except (TypeError, ValueError):
        return []
    return store.list_messages(self.conn, limit=n)
```

So any co-occurring `id`, `category` or `query` is silently discarded and the N NEWEST notes overall are returned.

Failure scenario A: «удали 3 заметки из crypto» routes to `{"count":3,"category":"crypto"}` — the three newest notes in the whole inbox go into the delete confirmation, none of them necessarily in crypto (the card lists #Ns, not categories, so the substitution is easy to miss). Failure scenario B: «убери #7 в архив» routed as `{"operation":"archive","id":7,"count":1}` archives the newest note instead of #7 — and do_note_lifecycle applies a single-row op immediately, with no confirmation.

This is the residual half of WP5's rule: an unparseable count was made fail-closed, but a count that shadows a named target was not.
